+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and method
+
+This review covers the customer-facing accounts API, payment dispatch and queue, account and transaction views,
+support records, statements, and closure workflow, including the four external integrations. It is based on the
+current source, `README.md`, `AGENTS.md`, configuration, fixtures, and API tests. It is a design/operational FMEA for
+the next release, not a production incident analysis.
+
+Scores use the usual 1–10 scale:
+
+- **Severity (S):** 1 = negligible; 10 = severe customer loss, duplicate payment, regulatory breach, or prolonged
+  service outage.
+- **Occurrence (O):** 1 = rare; 10 = expected or frequent under normal operation.
+- **Detection (D):** 1 = almost certain/early detection; 10 = unlikely to be detected before customer or dependent
+  impact.
+- **RPN:** `S × O × D`. The scores are relative and should be recalibrated with production incident and monitoring
+  data. RPNs of 100 or more should have an owned release action; any severity 9–10 item should be treated as a
+  priority even when its RPN is lower.
+
+## Failure-mode table
+
+| ID | Failure mode and likely cause | Effect on customers and dependent systems/teams | Detection | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| F-01 | **Payment is sent without fraud screening.** `FraudScreeningClient` exists, but `PaymentService`/`PaymentDispatcher` never calls it. | A fraudulent payment can reach the scheme and ledger, contrary to FRD-7. Customers may lose money; Fraud receives no `HELD_FOR_REVIEW` case and Operations/Finance must investigate after the fact. | Alert on payments with no screening decision; reconcile payment records against fraud decisions and scheme submissions. Add a release-blocking integration test. | 10 | 8 | 8 | **640** | Screen every payment before dispatch. Map `ACCEPT` to dispatch, `REFER` and timeout/error to `HELD_FOR_REVIEW` with no balance debit or scheme call, and `REJECT` to `REJECTED`/`FRAUD` without revealing the reason. Persist decision, timestamp, and correlation id. |
+| F-02 | **Fraud service outage or slow response is not fail-closed.** The configured 2-second client behavior is not connected to payment handling. | During a fraud outage, payments are either unscreened (if allowed through) or fail as a generic API error rather than being safely held. Customers get inconsistent outcomes; Fraud cannot review the held queue. | Synthetic timeout/error probes; metric for screening calls, decisions, latency, and payments lacking a decision; test `unavailable()` behavior. | 10 | 5 | 8 | **400** | Implement the explicit fail-closed path and bounded timeout, with a durable review queue, customer notification, and alert when held volume or age breaches the four-hour review target. |
+| F-03 | **Scheme timeout is treated as an exception.** `SchemeTimeoutException` is not caught by the dispatcher, although a timeout may mean the scheme accepted reference `P{id}`. | The request can roll back the local payment record while the payee may still be paid. A customer may retry, Operations cannot reconcile the missing record, and a retry with a new reference can pay twice. | Compare scheme settlement reports with local payments; alert on gateway timeouts and unmatched scheme references. | 10 | 5 | 9 | **450** | Persist `RECEIVED` before the external call, catch timeout, and resolve using the scheme status endpoint with the same `P{id}` reference. Make reconciliation and retry stateful and idempotent; never create a new scheme reference for the same payment. |
+| F-04 | **Ledger timeout leaves uncertain money state.** The dispatcher debits the local copy before posting and marks `POSTING_FAILED`; a timeout may mean the ledger booked the debit. | Customer may see a debited balance and `POSTING_FAILED`, or the ledger may contain a debit not represented correctly locally. An unsafe manual/automatic retry can debit twice; Finance reconciliation breaks and Operations must remediate. | Daily exception report, ledger-vs-local reconciliation by ledger reference, and alerts for `POSTING_FAILED` age/count. | 10 | 6 | 7 | **420** | Generate and persist `ledgerReference` before posting and reuse it for every retry. Add a ledger status/query or controlled idempotent repost workflow, record posting outcome, and reconcile scheme, ledger, and local status before customer completion. |
+| F-05 | **Ledger reference changes on a retry.** `PaymentDispatcher` calls `UUID.randomUUID()` on every dispatch, rather than preserving the original reference. | Reposting an uncertain payment can book the same customer debit twice. The customer loses money, Finance sees duplicate postings, and Operations cannot safely automate exception recovery. | Detect more than one ledger reference for a payment; match all ledger postings to payment id and scheme reference. | 10 | 4 | 8 | **320** | Set `ledgerReference` once when the payment is created or first dispatched, enforce uniqueness, and add a test that retries after an applied timeout using the same reference. |
+| F-06 | **External side effect and local transaction are not coordinated.** Scheme submission occurs inside the request transaction; an unchecked error after submission can roll back the payment row and status. | A payment can be accepted externally but disappear from the service. Customer history, support cases, daily limits, and Finance reconciliation are incomplete; customer retry is unsafe. | Monitor transaction rollbacks after integration calls; compare scheme instructions to local payment ids and alert on orphan references. | 10 | 5 | 8 | **400** | Use an outbox/state-machine design: commit the payment and intent first, dispatch asynchronously, and record every external outcome. Add recovery for any instruction with no local terminal status. |
+| F-07 | **Concurrent requests bypass balance or daily-limit checks.** Checks and the local debit are not protected by an account lock/atomic reservation; each request reads the balance copy. | Two payments can both pass validation and overspend or exceed the daily limit. Customers see an incorrect balance; ledger, scheme, Finance, and complaints teams see unexplained breaks. | Metrics for negative balances and daily-limit breaches; compare sum of in-flight/completed debits with ledger balance; concurrency tests. | 9 | 6 | 7 | **378** | Add optimistic/pessimistic locking or an atomic debit/reservation with a database constraint, and make payment creation idempotent for client retries. Reconcile rejected/parallel requests. |
+| F-08 | **The service balance copy is used as authoritative.** Payment validation reads `accounts.balance`, although the ledger overwrites it nightly and it can be stale during the day. | A payment may be incorrectly accepted or refused. Most seriously, closure could strand funds if it relies on the copy. Ledger is the source of truth; Finance and Operations must explain differences. | Scheduled balance comparison with ledger, age/last-sync metric, and alert on material variance. | 9 | 7 | 6 | **378** | For exact decisions (especially closure and high-value payment controls), query or reserve against the ledger. Track balance provenance and freshness; treat the copy as a display/cache with reconciliation. |
+| F-09 | **Queue sends on a bank holiday.** `WorkingDays` excludes only Saturday/Sunday; bank holidays are a stated gap. | A payment queued for a bank holiday is submitted with an invalid date and can be rejected with `DT01`, delaying the customer. Scheme, Operations, and support receive avoidable exceptions. | Alert on `DT01`, compare execution dates with the authoritative UK bank-holiday calendar, and monitor queued payments past due. | 8 | 6 | 6 | **288** | Use a versioned bank-holiday calendar shared with the scheme; calculate the next scheme working day and test holiday boundaries. Keep the payment queued on a date rejection that is retryable. |
+| F-10 | **Queued-payment job does not run or is not safely coordinated.** It is a single cron at 06:00, with no visible lock/claim, retry policy, or explicit missed-run handling. | Customers' payments remain `QUEUED` past their promised date. A duplicate job/instance can repeat dispatch and create uncertain ledger behavior. Operations' overdue queue grows; scheme and ledger timing commitments are missed. | Alert on queued payments past `executionDate`, job start/end/processed/failure metrics, and distributed-lock/leader-health monitoring. | 8 | 5 | 6 | **240** | Use a durable worker/outbox with row claiming and idempotent state transitions, distributed scheduling, bounded retries, and a replayable overdue queue. |
+| F-11 | **Notifications are coupled to dispatch outcome or are misleading.** The code catches notification runtime failures (good), but it always uses `PAYMENT_SENT` even for `POSTING_FAILED`, and there is no queued/under-review/failed notification path. | Customers may be told a payment was sent when ledger posting failed, or receive no message when a payment is queued/held. Support and complaints teams receive avoidable contacts; notifications cannot be trusted as a status signal. | Compare notification events with payment status; delivery/error metrics and customer reports; contract tests for each status. | 8 | 6 | 6 | **288** | Publish status-specific, durable notification events (`QUEUED`, `UNDER_REVIEW`, `FAILED`, `SENT`) after commit. Keep delivery best effort, retry in the notification subsystem, and never send `PAYMENT_SENT` for an unresolved posting. |
+| F-12 | **A notification integration call hangs or is misconfigured.** `HttpNotificationClient` has no configured timeout in `application.yml`; the dispatcher calls it synchronously. | Thread/request and transaction resources can be held, reducing availability. A slow notification service can make payment responses slow or cause cascading failures, even though notification is intended to be best effort. | Outbound latency, thread-pool saturation, timeout/error rate, and payment latency dashboards. | 7 | 5 | 6 | **210** | Configure a short timeout/circuit breaker and move notifications to an asynchronous outbox/queue so notification health cannot block or roll back payment processing. |
+| F-13 | **Duplicate client submission creates duplicate payments.** The API has no client idempotency key; a mobile retry after a timeout creates a new payment id and a new scheme reference. | The customer can pay the same bill twice. Ledger and scheme settlement contain two legitimate-looking payments; Finance and Complaints must reverse or investigate. | Detect repeated same-account/payee/amount/reference submissions in a short window; reconciliation by customer reference and scheme reference. | 9 | 5 | 8 | **360** | Require/support an idempotency key scoped to tenant and account, persist the request fingerprint and return the existing payment. Keep scheme and ledger references stable across retries. |
+| F-14 | **Payment API exposes full payee account details.** `PaymentView` returns payee sort code and full payee account number, despite the notification rule requiring last four digits and the wider data-protection sensitivity of payment data. | A compromised token, client log, screenshot, or downstream consumer exposes unnecessary payment-account data. Customers and payees face privacy harm; security, DPO, and complaints teams must handle a reportable incident. | API schema/privacy tests, DLP/log scanning, access-log review, and penetration testing. | 8 | 4 | 7 | **224** | Minimize responses to masked account details (or a one-time confirmation view), prohibit sensitive fields in logs/telemetry, and document a data-retention/access policy. |
+| F-15 | **Tenant or resource authorization regression exposes data.** Isolation depends on repeated service-layer `AccessPolicy` checks; a future endpoint or repository query can omit tenant filtering. | One bank brand could view another tenant's accounts, payments, notes, or customers. This is a confidentiality breach and damages all dependent staff and regulatory processes. | Cross-tenant authorization tests for every endpoint, audit logs with tenant/user/resource, anomaly alerts, and security review of repository methods. | 10 | 3 | 7 | **210** | Make tenant scoping mandatory in repository/service APIs, centralize authorization, add deny-by-default contract tests, and alert on cross-tenant query attempts. Preserve 404-for-invisible-resource behavior. |
+| F-16 | **Closure can be requested but cannot be safely completed.** The visible closure API creates and lists requests, but no approval/closure operation is implemented; the design also requires a ledger zero-balance check, not the copied balance. `ClosureRequest` uses `Instant.now()` rather than the injected clock. | Closures can be stuck indefinitely, or a future implementation may close an account with money remaining and strand funds. Customers wait; Approvers, Operations, Finance, and complaints teams lack an auditable completion path. Audit timestamps can be inconsistent in tests/operations. | Report pending requests by age; reconciliation of closed accounts against ledger zero; audit completeness checks; clock/time-source static analysis. | 9 | 5 | 8 | **360** | Implement an atomic two-person approval workflow, check the ledger's exact balance at approval and again at close, block pending payments, write immutable audit events, and inject `Clock` into all domain timestamps. |
+| F-17 | **Statements are stale, missing, or inconsistent with ledger closing balances.** The service stores statements but has no visible generation/cut process; monthly regulatory reporting depends on complete statements by the fifth working day. | Customers may receive incorrect statements; the regulatory return and Finance reconciliation can be wrong. Complaints teams lack reliable historical evidence. | Monthly completeness/reconciliation job, statement-versus-ledger totals, missing-period alert, and report sign-off by Finance. | 9 | 4 | 8 | **288** | Build an idempotent statement cut from ledger closing balances, with a calendar-aware deadline, immutable versioning, reconciliation totals, and an operational rerun/report. |
+| F-18 | **System-of-record data or service becomes unavailable.** The configured datasource is an in-memory H2 database with `create-drop` and fixture initialization; there is no visible production persistence/backup/restore posture. | Restart or deployment can remove accounts, payments, notes, cases, and closure history. Customers lose history and payment state; all support, Finance, Fraud, regulatory, and complaints dependents are disrupted. | Startup data-volume checks, persistence/restore drills, database health and backup monitoring, and alert on unexpected fixture initialization. | 10 | 3 | 8 | **240** | Confirm production uses durable, HA storage with migrations, backups, point-in-time recovery, encryption, restore testing, and deployment safeguards that cannot initialize fixture data. |
+| F-19 | **Clock/calendar logic is inconsistent.** Payment logic uses the injected UK `Clock`, but closure and case constructors call `Instant.now()` directly; cut-off and working-day behavior also lacks holiday rules. | Payments can be queued or sent on the wrong date around DST/cut-off/holiday boundaries; audit order and complaint evidence can be wrong. Customers and the scheme see missed promises. | Boundary tests around 15:30, DST, weekends, holidays; compare timestamps to a trusted time source; alert on late/early execution. | 8 | 5 | 6 | **240** | Inject one UK clock into all timestamps and scheduling decisions, use an authoritative business-calendar service, and add boundary tests for every release. |
+| F-20 | **Unhandled integration/protocol errors produce generic failures.** Scheme/ledger clients only translate selected exceptions; HTTP 5xx, malformed responses, and some connection errors can escape without a durable payment outcome. | Customers get 5xx/ambiguous results; local state may roll back while an external call may have succeeded. Support and Operations cannot distinguish retryable from terminal failure. | Structured integration error metrics, correlation IDs, dead-letter/outcome queue, and alerts on unexpected exception classes. | 9 | 5 | 7 | **315** | Define an error taxonomy and state transitions for every external response, catch/record unexpected errors, use circuit breakers/backoff, and require reconciliation before retrying ambiguous calls. |
+
+## Release priorities
+
+The immediate release gate should be F-01/F-02 (fraud screening), F-03/F-04/F-05/F-06 (ambiguous external side
+effects and idempotency), and F-07/F-08 (money-control races and stale balances). F-09/F-10 should be fixed before
+the first bank-holiday operating period. F-16 and F-17 require named Operations/Finance owners and an auditable
+runbook even if their API work is delivered separately.
+
+The minimum operational dashboard should show payment counts by status and age, fraud decision/latency/holds, scheme
+and ledger timeouts, stable payment-to-scheme/ledger references, queued and posting-failed backlog, balance and
+settlement reconciliation breaks, notification failures, job runs, and cross-tenant/security denials. Every alert
+should carry tenant, payment id, scheme reference, ledger reference, and a correlation id without logging full
+account numbers or sort codes.
